Found in a bug hunt, reproduced with two ticks started together and no
artificial widening; hit rate measured over ten rounds.

Check at Foundation.pm:413, acquire at :450. Between them _process_repo does
_sync_pull (network), a second disable check, _ref_hash and a full task
load. Two ticks that overlap -- the normal case, since a drain may run for
max_runtime (default 1800s) while cron fires every few minutes -- can both
pass the check.

_acquire_lock (Foundation/State.pm:44-55) then spews over whoever was there,
and _release_lock removes the file without ever comparing the pid, so the
first tick to finish unlocks the board for the one still running.

Reproduction:

    tick A pid 560905, tick B pid 560906
    t+6s: concurrent agents: 2
          .karr.lock says: 560906   (A=560905 B=560906)

Hit rate, ten rounds:

    no remote configured:            1, 1, 2, 2, 2
    with an origin (git pull inside the window): 2, 1, 1, 1, 2

Cost: two agents drain the same board simultaneously, racing each other's
CAS writes and claims, while one holds a lock file naming the other. Roughly
50% of genuinely simultaneous ticks in this measurement -- and a "tight
while-loop", which the POD recommends as one option, hits it constantly.
## Fix: .karr.lock is now a flock(2) on an open file descriptor the foundation keeps for the lifetime of the lock. _acquire_lock opens a fd and tries LOCK_EX|LOCK_NB; on EWOULDBLOCK it returns 0 immediately without overwriting the existing file. _release_lock closes the open fd (which drops the flock) and only unlinks the file if the recorded pid still matches $$. _lock_held is the flock check, not the kill(0, $pid) check, so a stale lock whose holder died is held=false and a fresh tick can take over without manual cleanup.

Regression test: t/162-foundation-lock-race.t. Four subtests: two ticks race, only the first wins; a refused _acquire_lock does not overwrite the recorded pid; _release_lock from a tick that does not hold the lock is a no-op; _lock_held reports false on an un-flocked .karr.lock regardless of pid text. All four pin the new contract.
